WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.313
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.313 --> 00:00:04.080
We're here at KubeCon
Barcelona with Bryan Liles.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.039
Brian is senior staff
engineer at VMware

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:12.300
and also the
co-chair of KubeCon.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.070
Bryan, can you talk
to us a little bit

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.250
about what that role is
and what it involves?

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.200
BRYAN LILES: So
the co-chairs, we

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.840
plan this whole entire
conference sans the vendor

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.170
stuff.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.677
So all the sessions,
all the keynotes,

00:00:28.677 --> 00:00:30.510
we coordinate the people
who do the reviews,

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.060
we make the final reviews,
we create the schedules,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.980
we get everybody in line.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.450
As you know, we sit down
with our keynote speakers,

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.452
and we help them
make better talks.

00:00:39.452 --> 00:00:41.160
Everything you see at
the conference that

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.928
makes you happy, we had a
hand in it, most likely.

00:00:43.928 --> 00:00:44.970
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.320
So how did you get
involved with this,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.195
and why did you think
that this was something

00:00:48.195 --> 00:00:49.750
that you really wanted to do?

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.623
BRYAN LILES: CNCF
contacted me last year.

00:00:52.623 --> 00:00:54.540
I had already done work
with Linux Foundation,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.510
helping planning the
OS summit for a while.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.770
And I think that they saw
that I was good on stage

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.050
and I was able to do
planning for conferences.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.480
It was actually a good
fit for a position

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.430
that opened up late last year.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.280
SAAD ALI: In terms of how you've
seen KubeCon evolve over time

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.953
and what's different at this
KubeCon, what do you see?

00:01:14.953 --> 00:01:17.120
BRYAN LILES: So one thing
we highlight this morning,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.200
there is 7,700 people here.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.920
This is literally the biggest
open-source conference

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.860
in Europe.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.085
And when we do our next
one in the United States,

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.960
it will be the biggest
open-source conference

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:28.740
in the world.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.930
That comes very
far from whenever

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.028
we met in San Francisco and
then when we did Berlin.

00:01:34.028 --> 00:01:35.820
SAAD ALI: How do you
make sure that this is

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.350
happening in a sustainable way?

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.183
BRYAN LILES: It's all
about the open source,

00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:40.310
not about your company.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.410
You work at Google.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.610
I work at VMware.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.000
But you notice that we're
not selling anything.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.810
We're basically talking
about the benefits

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.048
of the open-source community.

00:01:48.048 --> 00:01:49.590
Really, what this
conference is about

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.140
is showing what
everyone has to offer,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.692
offering open-source
content to our people,

00:01:54.692 --> 00:01:57.150
and making sure that it's just
not a sell fest because this

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.417
is not a vendor event.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:00.000
SAAD ALI: So it
sounds like the future

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.833
is moving beyond
Kubernetes and figuring out

00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:03.900
what we can do with
the building blocks

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.310
that Kubernetes has offered.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.060
BRYAN LILES: Yeah.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.380
I think that's actually it.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.630
Kubernetes was never supposed
to be end all be all.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.570
It's-- basically, it's
compute, network, and storage.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.040
Everything else needs
to be provided outside.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.610
And I'll speak for
VMware in a little bit

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.440
because we've been doing this
literally for 20 years now,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.300
basically the software
defined data center.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.310
What we're seeing
is now, growing

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.310
in this cloud-native way, it
just opens up so much space.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.260
And then looking
at what Google did,

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.780
Google starts in the
ad space, and then got

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.040
really good at compute.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.480
But now you're
seeing that we all

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.022
need to move in a
different direction

00:02:38.022 --> 00:02:41.340
because, unfortunately, none of
us have cracked this nut yet.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.155
And that's great.

00:02:42.155 --> 00:02:42.780
That's amazing.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.430
That means we're making progress
and we haven't stagnated yet.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:46.650
SAAD ALI: So one of
the really cool things

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.170
I've seen at the conference
was the mental health session.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.360
I think it was borne
out of the realization

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.340
that folks are starting to
burn out in the community.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.950
Can you talk a little
bit about that?

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.740
BRYAN LILES: When we did
Seattle back in December,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.520
we always have a
retrospective session.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.610
It's really the last
session, the last day,

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:03.820
anyone who wants to come.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.490
There's a lot of sentiment in
that room where this is hard,

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.870
and you want to go
to all the talks,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.590
and you want us to
meet all the people.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.440
And we're humans, and
we can't handle that.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.330
So we always were
paying attention to it.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.920
But Cheryl Hung, who
works with the CNCF,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.997
she actually went
the extra step.

00:03:21.997 --> 00:03:24.330
And they coordinated with
people, that they can actually

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.380
have a booth downstairs
in the sponsor showcase

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.868
that you could talk to
if you get burnt out.

00:03:29.868 --> 00:03:31.410
But I also want to
encourage anyone--

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.890
if you think about this,
a conference is just that.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.000
It's a conference.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.375
And there's going
to be-- there's

00:03:37.375 --> 00:03:39.270
going to be hallway
track, there's keynotes,

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.188
there's tutorials, and then
there's general sessions.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.980
The best thing about
a conference like this

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.208
is generally almost
everything is recorded.

00:03:46.208 --> 00:03:48.750
SAAD ALI: So, last question--
what are you looking forward to

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:49.772
at KubeCon?

00:03:49.772 --> 00:03:52.230
BRYAN LILES: So this year, what
I'm looking forward to is--

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.900
there's a lot of
software being announced

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.060
and people talking about
these projects that

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.990
go above and beyond.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.340
So what I'm looking for is
something that I didn't expect.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.172
And it's kind of weird to
say, but we go to these talks.

00:04:05.172 --> 00:04:06.630
And you're like,
oh someone's going

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:07.890
to talk about service mesh.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.515
Oh, I know you're
going to talk about--

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.190
I want someone to go
further than that,

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.940
and maybe take service
mesh, or anything else

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.019
even further than it is, or even
storage further than it is now.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.300
That's what I'm looking for.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.450
I'm looking for the
things that will probably

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.660
hit main line in the next year.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.690
But I want to hear about it now
so I can start following along.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:28.440
SAAD ALI: Awesome.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:29.590
Thank you very
much, Bryan Liles.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:30.548
BRYAN LILES: All right.

00:04:30.548 --> 00:04:31.690
Thank you, Saad.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:35.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

